.TH SYSCALL_REPLAYER 1 "September 2026" "ebpf-syscall" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
syscall_replayer \- replay IO-related system calls from a JSON capture
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B syscall_replayer
.RI [ JSON_FILE ]
.RB [ \-\-paced ]
.RB [ \-\-serial | \-\-per-process ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B syscall_replayer
reads detailed JSON made by
.BR syscall_monitor (1)
and recreates supported open, mapping, read, write, synchronization, and close
operations.  It maintains captured-to-replay descriptor mappings and verifies
operation results where supported.
.PP
The default input is
.IR final_replay.json .
The default execution mode assigns a worker to each captured process so
independent producers can issue IO concurrently.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-paced
Delay operations relative to a shared monotonic start so their issue times
follow the captured timestamps.  Without this option, workers run as quickly
as dependencies allow.
.TP
.B \-\-per-process
Use one replay worker per captured process.  This is the default.
.TP
.B \-\-serial
Use the legacy single-worker path.  This changes concurrency and is useful for
controlled comparisons, not for preserving a concurrent workload.
.SH FIDELITY
This tool reproduces application-facing calls.  It cannot guarantee the same
device command stream because the page cache, readahead, writeback, filesystem,
block layer, and scheduler can transform those calls.  When device fidelity is
the claim, capture with
.BR nvme_tp_monitor (1),
export with
.BR "kvio iolog" ,
and re-record the replay.
.SH SAFETY
Replay can create, overwrite, map, and modify files named in the capture.  Run
only after reviewing the JSON and redirecting paths to disposable storage.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR syscall_monitor (1),
.BR kvio (1),
.BR nvme_tp_monitor (1)
